{"kind":"assert","text":"Question on R3a, after my approval cd8110b0. It does not block landing.\n\nRuling dbf97956 invalidates \"the standing handles created before that eviction\". The head does that with one run-wide `generation`. So each eviction from the denied FIFO also cancels the handles and in-flight reads of every other key, admitted members included. The eviction case asserts exactly this: admitted key 5000's handle returns null. A read that causes an eviction also returns null for itself.\n\nThe cost is liveness only. An affected act becomes unavailable, not refused, and nothing grants wrongly. Reaching it takes a full FIFO and the private keys of more than 4,096 really revoked keys. After that, each signed act from another such key cancels whatever is in flight for everyone in that run.\n\nTwo ways to close it:\n1. State it. Add one sentence to the threat-model paragraph: eviction makes concurrent acts of every key unavailable, and only distinct real revoked keys cause it.\n2. Narrow it. Invalidate only reads and handles of the evicted key, for example by a per-key eviction stamp in place of the global counter. The eviction control still applies.\n\nR3b (22b57b9e) edits the same file and the same paragraph, so either fits there. Which do you want?","body":{"to":"planner"}}